Jahshaka patch 0079 — the final grade's 8-bit write is DITHERED.

MEDIA-only patch (`Samples/Media/2.0/scripts/materials/HDR/GLSL/FinalToneMapping_ps.glsl`
and `Samples/Media/2.0/scripts/materials/HDR/HDR.material`). THE PROCESS COST IS
STATED RATHER THAN AVOIDED: Studio stages its media from the SOURCE tree
(cmake/IncludeOgre.cmake), so every tree must reset its ogre-next submodule and
re-run `irisgl/scripts/build-ogre.sh` — no engine recompile beyond the staging,
but the loop must run. It overlaps NO other patch in the stack (nothing else
touches either file), so the reverse-check has no blind spot to trip over here.

IT DEPENDS ON JAHSHAKA MEDIA: the dither itself lives in
`irisgl/engine/media/Hlms/Jahshaka/JahDither.glsl` — ours, not upstream's —
and this patch only includes it. Ogre's own `#include` (enable_include_header,
OgreHighLevelGpuProgram::parseIncludeFile) resolves the name through the
resource GROUP, and `Hlms/Jahshaka` is registered in the same group as the HDR
materials (OgreEngine::registerCommonMaterials), so one file serves every
shader that will ever need it. A tree with the patch and no staged Jahshaka
media fails LOUD at shader compile; a tree with the media and no patch renders
the old banded picture and says so once in the log (chain::setDither).

WHY (Jahshaka lane DITHER-1, 2026-09-18, measured; the owner's smoke report on
push #50 and ledger §768).

THE DEFECT. The owner reported "faint circular rippling in the ground plane
while flying". It is 8-BIT CONTOUR BANDING of a smooth shading gradient. The
renderer computes in RGBA16F; the window, the offscreen render target and the
VR eye image are all 8-bit UNORM, and 10-bit output is not available on this
path (the X surface offers B8G8R8A8 only — LATER_OPTIMISATIONS L14). A smooth
gradient therefore lands on a staircase of display codes, the contours sit
where the gradient crosses a code, and they SLIDE with the camera. There was no
dither anywhere in the final grade — only inside the SSR shaders. Every
renderer dithers its final output, at any bit depth, for exactly this reason.

THE 73 CODES THE OLD LDR BUFFER LOST START AT 75 — 75, 83, 89, 94, 98, 102,
106, 110, 113, 117, 120, 123, 126, 129, 132, 135, 137, 140, 143, 145 … thinning
to every other code only near the top — so a mid-grey ground sits in the worst
of it. (That is the kLdr format fix, which rides in the same commit but is C++,
not this patch; it is named here because it is half of the owner's symptom.)

THE MEASUREMENT (default scene, plain-colour ground, camera 25 m straight down,
'scene' grade, 1920x1080, the middle half of the frame):

                                      longest run   residual   radial profile
                                      of one code   noise      max annulus jump
  before                                  475 px    0.024 LSB      0.5756 LSB
  after (this patch)                       24 px    0.346 LSB      0.0237 LSB

The radial profile IS the owner's rippling — concentric annuli of the picture,
averaged — and its step falls by a factor of 24.

WHY THE TONEMAP QUAD AND NOWHERE ELSE. `HDR/FinalToneMapping` is the one place
in this engine where a floating-point picture becomes display codes. Every
target it writes is 8-bit UNORM — the window, the offscreen RTT, the VR eye
image, the LDR buffer SMAA works on, the looks stage's first buffer, the
picture-in-picture inset — so the dither here is UNCONDITIONAL and needs no
per-view flag (which it could not have: this material is a MaterialManager
singleton shared by every view). Nothing downstream needs one: SMAA's
neighbourhood blend and the looks stage read a picture that is ALREADY
quantised, and the dither is built so that a value already sitting exactly on a
code rounds back to that code (amplitude 0.498 of a code, not 0.500), so a
second dithered write over an already-quantised picture is the identity.

WHY IT IS ADDED TO THE SHADER'S OWN OUTPUT VALUE, UNSCALED. In this engine the
tonemapper's output IS the display code (measured — IEditorViewport::
ScreenshotGrade), and the targets are plain UNORM, so `fragColour` is what the
hardware rounds. A dither belongs on the quantiser's own axis.

WHY THE SHADER QUANTISES ITSELF, AND WHY THE NOISE IS INTEGER ARITHMETIC. The
picture must not be decided by the driver. A float `fract(52.9829189 * fract(
dot(p, k)))` is evaluated with whatever fused-multiply-add contraction the
compiler chooses, and a `fract` of a large product is catastrophically
sensitive to that last bit — a 1-ULP difference gives a completely different
offset, not a nearby one — while handing a float to a UNORM attachment lets the
HARDWARE break the rounding tie. Both would put NVIDIA, MoltenVK and lavapipe
on different pictures. So the offset is computed in 32-bit unsigned integer
arithmetic, whose wraparound is exact and mandated (the same interleaved
gradient noise, its constants held as Q32 fixed point), keyed on the INTEGER
pixel coordinate; and the shader does its own `floor(v * 255 + 0.5 + n) / 255`
so the hardware receives a value already exactly on a code. The residual is
stated honestly in the header: a pixel whose pre-floor sum sits within one ULP
of an integer can still fall either side, because the value reaching the dither
is the filmic curve's float output, which this patch does not own and never
did. The claim is that the DITHER adds no disagreement of its own.

THE CODE DEPTH IS A NAMED CONSTANT (JAH_DITHER_CODE_MAX, 255.0) because the
amplitude is half a code: a 10-bit target reached through this material would
need its own depth and its own, smaller, amplitude. Nothing selects one today.

THE UNIFORM. `jahDitherOff` is a DIAGNOSTIC override (JAHSHAKA_NO_DITHER), and
its name says "off" so that its SAFE value is zero: a constant buffer nobody has
written renders the CORRECT picture, and a garbage value is clamped to the
picture this engine drew before the dither existed. Neither failure can produce
noise. It is what makes the one-process A/B in `hdr.dither` possible, and the
selftest-hash comparison with it. (No line of this header may BEGIN with two
dashes: `git apply` reads such a line as a file header and refuses the whole
patch.)

WHAT IT MOVES. The selftest's two poses move by exactly 1/255 on 24.79 % and
21.14 % of their pixels and by nothing more; the same binary with
JAHSHAKA_NO_DITHER=1 reproduces the pre-lane hashes byte for byte.

APPLIES TO the pinned ogre-next; verified with `git apply --check` against the
pristine pin and `git apply --reverse --check` after applying.
diff --git a/Samples/Media/2.0/scripts/materials/HDR/GLSL/FinalToneMapping_ps.glsl b/Samples/Media/2.0/scripts/materials/HDR/GLSL/FinalToneMapping_ps.glsl
index c37dad61f..5fa8ac74e 100644
--- a/Samples/Media/2.0/scripts/materials/HDR/GLSL/FinalToneMapping_ps.glsl
+++ b/Samples/Media/2.0/scripts/materials/HDR/GLSL/FinalToneMapping_ps.glsl
@@ -48,6 +48,25 @@ vulkan_layout( ogre_t2 ) uniform texture2D bloomRt;
 vulkan( layout( ogre_s0 ) uniform sampler samplerPoint );
 vulkan( layout( ogre_s2 ) uniform sampler samplerBilinear );
 
+// JAHSHAKA (patch 0079, lane DITHER-1): the dither that makes this quad's
+// 8-bit write honest. This is THE place a floating-point picture becomes
+// display codes in this engine -- every target this material ever writes is
+// 8-bit UNORM (the window, the offscreen render target, the VR eye image, the
+// LDR buffer SMAA works on, the looks stage's first buffer, the
+// picture-in-picture inset) -- so the dither is unconditional here and needed
+// nowhere else. Why, how big and why it is keyed on the pixel alone: the
+// header, which is Jahshaka media and is found through the resource group.
+#include "JahDither.glsl"
+vulkan( layout( ogre_P0 ) uniform Params { )
+	// THE SAFE DEFAULT IS ZERO, i.e. DITHERED: a zero-filled constant buffer
+	// (a frame drawn before the host has pushed anything, and any target
+	// this material reaches through a path that does not push) renders the
+	// CORRECT picture rather than a banded one, and a garbage value is
+	// clamped to "off", i.e. to the picture this engine drew before the
+	// dither existed. Neither failure can produce noise.
+	uniform float jahDitherOff;	// 0 = dither (normal), 1 = JAHSHAKA_NO_DITHER
+vulkan( }; )
+
 void main()
 {
 	float fInvLumAvg = texture( vkSampler2D( lumRt, samplerPoint ), vec2( 0.0, 0.0 ) ).x;
@@ -61,5 +80,13 @@ void main()
 	//vSample.xyz  = vSample.xyz / (1 + vSample.xyz); //Reinhard Simple
 	vSample.xyz  = ( vSample.xyz - 0.5 ) * 1.25 + 0.5 + 0.11;
 
+	// The INTEGER pixel, which is the dither's only key. gl_FragCoord.xy
+	// carries the half-pixel centre offset, so the truncation is what
+	// names the pixel; the y origin differs between the GL and Vulkan
+	// paths and the noise does not care, since it is keyed on the pixel
+	// and not on a direction.
+	vSample.xyz = jahDither8( vSample.xyz, ivec2( gl_FragCoord.xy ),
+							  1.0 - clamp( jahDitherOff, 0.0, 1.0 ) );
+
 	fragColour = vSample;
 }
